WEBVTT

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.089
A very warm welcome to Part 2 of

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:09.464
this lecture on energy and climate change.

00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:11.114
Last week,

00:00:11.114 --> 00:00:12.975
we discussed climate change

00:00:12.975 --> 00:00:14.579
and its background.

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:17.850
We discussed a pressing need to tackle

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.445
climate change and ensure

00:00:20.445 --> 00:00:22.950
energy access to all;

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.319
the need for social inclusivity,

00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:29.449
and equal opportunities for all.

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:33.019
Then we discussed our first potential plan

00:00:33.019 --> 00:00:34.910
to tackle climate change

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:38.300
that included two main measures,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.850
mitigation and adaptation.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.639
Lastly, we discussed

00:00:43.639 --> 00:00:46.609
two main technology-focused components

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:49.550
to foster emission mitigation:

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.320
energy efficiency,

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.180
and clean energy generation.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.330
We will start this lecture with

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.625
a potential plan number two:

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:03.980
policy instruments.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.439
Policy instruments can help us

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:08.895
mitigate CO2 emissions,

00:01:08.895 --> 00:01:10.720
accelerate the adoption of

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.384
clean energy and

00:01:12.384 --> 00:01:15.409
energy-efficient technologies.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.840
I've been mainly discussing

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.390
four types of policy measures.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.180
The first two, institutional

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.205
and regulatory measures,

00:01:27.205 --> 00:01:29.980
are traditional approaches that we have been

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.205
using to control all kinds of emissions,

00:01:33.205 --> 00:01:35.230
be it ozone emissions or

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.049
SOx or NOx and so many others.

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:40.239
These approaches have worked

00:01:40.239 --> 00:01:42.715
well for us so far.

00:01:42.715 --> 00:01:46.779
However, the issue of CO2 emissions is much

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:48.970
more complex than what we

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.955
have dealt with so far.

00:01:51.955 --> 00:01:56.110
It is an unprecedented global issue.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:59.259
It involves every person, all businesses,

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:00.685
companies, institutions,

00:02:00.685 --> 00:02:02.920
every section of the economy.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.229
Every action in the economy

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:08.275
includes greenhouse gas emissions.

00:02:08.275 --> 00:02:10.525
Traditional policy instruments

00:02:10.525 --> 00:02:12.564
might not be enough.

00:02:12.564 --> 00:02:16.180
We need something more, something innovative.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.075
These last two policy instruments,

00:02:19.075 --> 00:02:20.710
flexibility and

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:23.530
economic incentive-based measures,

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.605
are some of such innovative approaches.

00:02:26.605 --> 00:02:30.849
My focus would be to discuss these two.

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:34.000
I will also briefly introduce the

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.114
first two policy measures.

00:02:37.114 --> 00:02:41.285
The first one - by using institutional tools,

00:02:41.285 --> 00:02:46.000
we aim to internalise the negative impacts of emissions.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.169
A  simple example

00:02:48.169 --> 00:02:51.184
is in specifying liabilities.

00:02:51.184 --> 00:02:55.205
Authorities can specify liabilities.

00:02:55.205 --> 00:02:58.084
If the public gets harmed by pollution,

00:02:58.084 --> 00:03:01.175
they have a right to sue polluters.

00:03:01.175 --> 00:03:02.854
Another example is

00:03:02.854 --> 00:03:06.199
the development of social responsibility.

00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:08.659
If you go to buy electrical appliances

00:03:08.659 --> 00:03:11.089
in Singapore, for example,

00:03:11.089 --> 00:03:13.820
an air-con or a refrigerator,

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.279
you will notice some green ticks on it.

00:03:17.279 --> 00:03:19.090
It is part of

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:22.869
Singapore's mandatory energy labelling scheme.

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:26.139
These green ticks tell you how

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:28.404
energy-efficient your air-con

00:03:28.404 --> 00:03:30.024
or refrigerator is.

00:03:30.024 --> 00:03:32.079
The more ticks, the better.

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:34.869
But of course, as we discussed,

00:03:34.869 --> 00:03:36.700
the upfront cost of

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.609
energy-efficient devices is higher.

00:03:39.609 --> 00:03:40.630
If you go for

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.849
a more energy-efficient air-con,

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:45.289
you will be paying a higher price.

00:03:45.289 --> 00:03:46.899
In the longer term,

00:03:46.899 --> 00:03:48.759
you will ultimately save

00:03:48.759 --> 00:03:51.565
money on electricity bills.

00:03:51.565 --> 00:03:55.000
Most importantly, you'll be contributing to

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.934
reducing CO2 emissions

00:03:56.934 --> 00:03:59.339
and mitigating climate change.

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:00.890
That is a way of

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:03.289
developing social responsibility.

00:04:03.289 --> 00:04:05.810
The government asks the manufacturer

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.060
to disclose this information so

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.514
consumers can make an informed decision.

00:04:12.514 --> 00:04:14.390
The next one is

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:15.530
the regulatory or

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.065
command and control approach.

00:04:18.065 --> 00:04:19.655
Under this approach,

00:04:19.655 --> 00:04:22.549
government or regulatory authorities

00:04:22.549 --> 00:04:26.345
take direct control over polluters.

00:04:26.345 --> 00:04:28.535
They ask them to stop polluting,

00:04:28.535 --> 00:04:30.665
or pollute below a limit,

00:04:30.665 --> 00:04:32.270
or sell something that

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:35.704
meets certain performance benchmarks.

00:04:35.704 --> 00:04:37.670
Singapore's minimum

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.620
energy performance standards

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.079
are one such example.

00:04:42.079 --> 00:04:43.940
In the previous example,

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.815
we talked about green ticks

00:04:45.815 --> 00:04:47.974
on electrical appliances.

00:04:47.974 --> 00:04:51.095
From 2022 onwards,

00:04:51.095 --> 00:04:52.715
for certain appliances,

00:04:52.715 --> 00:04:55.414
the minimum will be two ticks.

00:04:55.414 --> 00:04:58.459
Manufacturers or suppliers can

00:04:58.459 --> 00:05:00.245
no longer sell appliances




